WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.780
What are Co-linear and Co-planar points?

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.040
How many lines can we draw through one point?

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.060
We can draw one like this,

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.520
another one like this and many more!

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.240
Infinite lines can be drawn through one point.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.140
What about two points?

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.920
How many lines can be drawn passing through both these points?

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.740
Just one!

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.280
One and only one line

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.540
can be drawn passing through two given points.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.800
Now here's an interesting one.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.760
How many lines can be drawn passing through three given points?

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.420
Okay, look at these three points.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.380
We cannot draw a single line passing through all three of these points.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.240
But, if we have three points placed like this,

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.100
were exactly one line passes through them,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.300
then the points are called co-linear points.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.280
Remember three points are co-linear,

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.860
if exactly one line passes through each of them.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.680
Then what are these three points called?

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.400
Simple, they are called non co-linear points.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.860
Co-linear if one line passes through them.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.480
Non Co-linear if a line does not pass through them.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:26.440
So wait, three collinear points are special. They form a line.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.340
But what's special about three non co-linear points?

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.680
Three non co-linear points form exactly one plane.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.940
If we have three non co-linear points like this,

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.840
exactly one plane will pass through them.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.540
Will four or more non co-linear points always form a plane?

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.440
Not necessarily! But if they do, they are called Co-planar points.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.100
Understand these concepts well.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.860
Three points are co-linear

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.980
if exactly one line passes through them.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.580
Exactly one plane passes through three non co-linear points.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.680
And a four or more points form a plane

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.080
they are called co-planar.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.480
Co-planar is basically one plane.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.060
So when are two or more lines Co-planar?

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.320
Easy! If the lines lie on one plane, they are co-planar.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.020
Two or more lines lying on the same plane

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.420
are called Co-planar lines.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.420
When are two lines not co- planar?

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.000
If two lines do not lie on the same plane like this,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.700
then they are non co-planar.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.740
And we look at one last combination of a line and a point.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.980
Will they lie on one plane?

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.840
Yes, a line and a point will always lie on a plane.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.980
Exactly one plane.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.180
It doesn't matter how you position the point or the line.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.720
You can move them both as you wish.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.380
You will always have one plane passing through them.

